WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.389
Least privilege and input validation.

2
00:00:02.669 --> 00:00:07.320
Least privilege means each tool gets the narrowest access that does its job.

3
00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:23.195
OWASP’s examples are concrete: a tool that only needs to read a database should not connect with an identity that can also update and delete, and a tool acting for one user should not use a generic high-privilege account that can see everyone’s files.

4
00:00:23.475 --> 00:00:31.603
For the meeting-notes tool, create_task should hold a token that can create tasks in one project, not an admin key to your whole tracker.

5
00:00:31.883 --> 00:00:40.309
Anthropic’s injection guidance makes the same point from the other side: limit access so that a successful attack can do minimal damage.

6
00:00:40.589 --> 00:00:42.531
Input validation has two layers.

7
00:00:42.811 --> 00:00:44.219
The first is the schema.

8
00:00:44.499 --> 00:00:54.931
With strict: true, the API constrains generation so tool inputs match your JSON Schema: correct types, required fields present, enum values respected.

9
00:00:55.211 --> 00:01:00.032
That removes a whole class of bugs, such as a string “2” where you needed the number 2.

10
00:01:00.312 --> 00:01:05.261
The second layer is yours, because a schema cannot know what exists or what is allowed.

11
00:01:05.541 --> 00:01:07.184
Is this email a teammate?

12
00:01:07.464 --> 00:01:10.429
Is this due date in the future, and not three years out?

13
00:01:10.709 --> 00:01:13.248
Is this project one this user can write to?

14
00:01:13.528 --> 00:01:19.181
The MCP specification puts it bluntly: servers must validate all tool inputs.

15
00:01:19.461 --> 00:01:23.685
Run both layers on every call, including calls you expect to approve.
